# Burla Worktrees and Dev Clusters

For Burla tasks, never edit the primary checkout. Create or reopen a linked
worktree for the task and edit there.

One worktree gets one dev cluster. Everything that would otherwise be shared
(docker network, container labels, head port, node ports, head state, relay
subdomain, EC2 node tags) is namespaced by the worktree name automatically, so
several agents can run clusters side by side on one machine. Run
`make cluster-info` to see a worktree's cluster name and dashboard URL; never
assume the head is on port 5001.

Start a cluster from its worktree with `make local-dev` (whole cluster local, for
light work: frontend, dashboard, head/API behavior) or `make remote-dev` (head
local, nodes are real EC2 in the Burla test AWS account, for real scale or
real-VM behavior, or when this machine cannot take another local cluster).
Development happens in AWS; GCP is production-only. Read the
`burla-parallel-dev` skill before starting or stopping a cluster.

Tear down with `make stop` (label-scoped to that worktree) or `make stop-all`.
Never `docker rm` containers by `node_*` / `worker_*` name prefix: that destroys
other agents' clusters.

In remote-dev, node VMs run the worktree's current branch from GitHub, so
`node_service` / `worker_server.py` changes must be committed and pushed before
they reach a node. `main_service` changes are always live in both modes.

When a task is done, push its branch and merge it into `dev`, then stop the
cluster. Keep the worktree until it is no longer needed; if it is dirty, commit
the work on its branch with a clear WIP message rather than discarding it.
